{
  "id": "insurance",
  "title": "Digital motor insurance claims",
  "case_type": "insurance",
  "scenario": "Claims director: We want to replace our paper-heavy motor insurance claims process with a digital claims platform. Policyholders should be able to report an accident from their phone, upload photos of the damage and the police report, and follow the status of their claim. Simple claims should be settled very quickly. Surveyor manager: Our surveyors inspect vehicles at partner garages and need to record their assessment and repair estimate in the platform from a tablet, even where the network is poor. Fraud and investigations team: Claims with warning signs, such as a policy bought a few days before the accident or repeated claims on the same vehicle, should be flagged for investigation before any payment is made. Finance: Approved claims are paid directly to the garage or to the policyholder's bank account; payments above two lakh rupees need a second approval. IT architect: Policy data stays in our fifteen-year-old policy administration system, which we cannot replace for at least three years. We plan to deliver the platform in stages, starting with motor claims and adding health and travel claims later. Compliance officer: Health and personal data in claims must be protected and used only for handling the claim. Claim documents must be kept for eight years. The insurance regulator expects every claim to be acknowledged within one working day of being reported. Customer service head: Policyholders want to talk to a person when they need to, and all communication should be in their preferred language. Data team: Management wants all claims data kept indefinitely so that it can be used for analytics.",
